    # from an older phase's printed log carries that phase's stale claims). What
    # changed, and why:
    #  1. The names are part 3's, and the spec cited is specs/0010.
    #  2. The base is part 2's close-out doc commit, resolved from git by its
    #     subject, where part 2's resolved part 1's. As before, it is read from git
    #     and never typed, and the pattern is now also asserted to match exactly one
    #     commit (part 2's doc-rulings commit 9bd9102 was given a subject that does
    #     not match it, and this checks that rather than assuming it).
    #  3. THE PHASE COMMIT IS GIVEN TO THIS SCRIPT and compared with HEAD, with two
    #     controls that refuse a fabricated commit, both values printed: the rule
    #     specs/0001 gained from M1 part 3 P1 ruling 11, after part 2's close-out
    #     ran. Part 2's script read main and HEAD and printed them, which asserts
    #     nothing about which tree the log describes.
    #  4. THE ORDER DEPARTS FROM PART 2'S, by the maintainer's choice typed in the
    #     implementing session on 2026-09-21. Part 2 ran both parts of this log, then
    #     the CI run, then the scan, and made one record commit after the push. Here
    #     part 1 runs now, the scan follows it, and one record commit holds the two;
    #     the push itself (part 2 of this script) runs only on planning's word and
    #     the maintainer's authorisation, and its output and the CI run go in a
    #     later log and record commit. So the commits this part lists as "to push"
    #     are those at HEAD now; the record commit that holds this log is pushed with
    #     them and is not in the list, since this log cannot name the commit that
    #     holds it. Part 2 of the script reads main afresh and checks against the
    #     base recorded here.
    #  5. The gate still has nine steps and the expected stdout is unchanged: part 3
    #     changed scripts/gate-self-test.sh (P1) but not scripts/check.sh.
    #
    # Usage: M1CLOSE_LIB=<lib> m1p3close-push.sh pre <phase commit> > <scratch file>
    #          step 2: the environment, hook and core.hooksPath checks and the full
    #          gate; records the base (origin/main) and the count of commits to
    #          push. NO PUSH, and this mode can never push.
    #        M1CLOSE_LIB=<lib> m1p3close-push.sh push <base> >> <scratch file>
    #          steps 3 and 4: refuses unless origin/main still equals <base>,
    #          pushes, and checks the hook's range, the count and git's report
    #          against <base> as recorded in part 1 of the log, never against
    #          origin/main read after the push (the T0b close-out's ruling: "a push
    #          script records the base before the push and compares against that").
    #
    # THE MAINTAINER'S AUTHORISATION. specs/0003-ci.md, the section on the push,
    # requires three preconditions: the phase DONE, planning's word, and the
    # maintainer typing authorisation in the implementing session. Part 2 of this
    # log is only ever run after that sentence has been typed. Planning's word does
    # not stand in for it, and neither does this script.
    #
    # Every commit id this log reports is read from git at run time by a command
    # the log prints; none is typed.
    set -uo pipefail
    cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)" || exit 2
    
    MODE=${1:?pre or push}
    LIB=${M1CLOSE_LIB:?the path to the counting library is required}
    . "$LIB"
    REPO=thekeoni1/emend
    URL=https://github.com/thekeoni1/emend.git
    BASE_PATTERN='^M1 part 2 close-out: commit planning doc updates'
    export GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 RUSTUP_AUTO_INSTALL=0 CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never
    T=$(mktemp -d)
    trap 'rm -rf "$T"' EXIT
    fail=0
    
    show() { echo "$1:"; sed 's/^/    /' "$2"; }
    check() { echo "$1: $2"; [ "$2" = yes ] || fail=1; }
    yn() { if "$@"; then echo yes; else echo no; fi; }
    # exit_and_bytes <exit> <bytes> <want exit> <want bytes>: both match, in one
    # function so neither condition is dropped.
    exit_and_bytes() { [ "$1" -eq "$3" ] && [ "$2" = "$4" ]; }
    # same_nonempty <a> <b>: both set and equal.
    same_nonempty() { [ -n "$1" ] && [ "$1" = "$2" ]; }
    # holds_once <file> <line>: the file holds that whole line exactly once.
    holds_once() { [ "$(count_matches -F "$2" "$1")" = 1 ]; }
    # tree_clean: git status ran and printed nothing.
    tree_clean() { [ "$(run_lines git status --porcelain)" = 0 ]; }
    # files_listed <n>: the range's file list was read and is not empty.
